1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,882
Three beakers, on the same bench, in the same room.

2
00:00:03,882 --> 00:00:09,134
Fifty grams of crushed ice, one hundred grams, and one hundred and fifty grams.

3
00:00:09,367 --> 00:00:11,621
The ice is crushed the same way.

4
00:00:11,621 --> 00:00:13,223
The beakers are identical.

5
00:00:13,223 --> 00:00:15,176
The room does not change.

6
00:00:15,176 --> 00:00:18,414
Only one thing is different: how much ice there is.

7
00:00:18,633 --> 00:00:20,848
Now we start the clock and let them melt.

8
00:00:20,848 --> 00:00:22,840
Which one finishes first?

9
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:25,926
And be careful, that is not the real question.

10
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:29,179
The real question is: how much longer will the big one take?

11
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,823
Fifty grams: four minutes.

12
00:00:42,823 --> 00:00:45,402
One hundred grams: eight minutes.

13
00:00:45,402 --> 00:00:48,331
One hundred and fifty grams: twelve minutes.

14
00:00:48,331 --> 00:00:49,404
Exactly in step.

15
00:00:49,633 --> 00:00:50,533
Look at that.

16
00:00:50,533 --> 00:00:52,577
Double the mass, double the time.

17
00:00:52,577 --> 00:00:54,895
Triple the mass, triple the time.

18
00:00:54,895 --> 00:00:57,433
The time is proportional to the mass.

19
00:00:57,667 --> 00:01:00,260
And notice what made that conclusion possible.

20
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:05,143
We changed one thing only, and kept everything else the same.

21
00:01:05,143 --> 00:01:08,215
That single habit is the whole of this unit.

22
00:01:08,215 --> 00:01:14,197
Today you learn the method, the safety, the experiment, the survey, and the measurement that go with it.

23
00:01:18,1000 --> 00:01:23,689
This is Class Eight Science and Technology, Unit One: Scientific Learning.

24
00:01:26,067 --> 00:01:27,644
Seven stops on the way.

25
00:01:27,644 --> 00:01:29,220
The scientific method.

26
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:30,352
Variables.

27
00:01:30,352 --> 00:01:32,006
Laboratory safety.

28
00:01:32,006 --> 00:01:33,556
Doing the experiment.

29
00:01:33,556 --> 00:01:34,949
Research work.

30
00:01:34,949 --> 00:01:36,264
Running a survey.

31
00:01:36,264 --> 00:01:37,680
And measurement and units.

32
00:01:42,033 --> 00:01:45,420
Let us begin with the hardest idea in this chapter.

33
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:48,324
Science is not a pile of facts in a book.

34
00:01:48,324 --> 00:01:50,177
It is a way of finding out what is true.

35
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:54,907
And here is the test that every scientific claim has to pass.

36
00:01:54,907 --> 00:01:58,058
Can it be checked by an observation or a measurement?

37
00:01:58,058 --> 00:02:02,925
If it cannot be tested at all, it is not science, however beautiful it sounds.

38
00:02:03,133 --> 00:02:09,267
Take the first claim: a bigger glass of water takes longer to freeze in the same freezer.

39
00:02:09,267 --> 00:02:10,153
Testable.

40
00:02:10,153 --> 00:02:11,877
We can weigh the water and time it.

41
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:17,844
Now the second: water that is spoken to kindly freezes into prettier ice.

42
00:02:17,844 --> 00:02:19,445
How would you measure prettier?

43
00:02:19,445 --> 00:02:20,682
Who decides?

44
00:02:20,682 --> 00:02:25,275
There is no observation that could settle it, so it is not a scientific statement.

45
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,745
Now the working order that scientists follow.

46
00:02:28,745 --> 00:02:30,965
Seven steps, and then round again.

47
00:02:31,167 --> 00:02:32,874
One: observe.

48
00:02:32,874 --> 00:02:35,622
Notice something, and describe it exactly.

49
00:02:35,622 --> 00:02:40,918
Not that the ice melts fast, but that the ice melted in fifteen minutes in the sun.

50
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:44,562
Two: turn it into a question a measurement can answer.

51
00:02:44,767 --> 00:02:47,268
Three: give a testable answer before you start.

52
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:49,599
Four: say what you will see if you are right.

53
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:53,978
Five: change one thing, keep the rest the same, and measure.

54
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:58,876
Six: put the readings in a table, then a graph, and look for a pattern.

55
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:01,823
Seven: was the hypothesis supported?

56
00:03:01,823 --> 00:03:02,501
Then repeat it.

57
00:03:02,733 --> 00:03:05,039
And then the arrow that matters most.

58
00:03:05,039 --> 00:03:10,834
If the result does not support the hypothesis, you do not throw the experiment away.

59
00:03:10,834 --> 00:03:13,214
You go back, and you ask a better question.

60
00:03:13,433 --> 00:03:16,143
A failed hypothesis is still a result.

61
00:03:16,143 --> 00:03:18,278
It is often the most useful one you will get.

62
00:03:22,767 --> 00:03:30,716
Every experiment has exactly three kinds of quantity in it, and mixing them up is the most common mistake in school science.

63
00:03:30,933 --> 00:03:34,997
The independent variable is the one you choose and change.

64
00:03:34,997 --> 00:03:40,563
In our ice race, that was the mass of the ice: fifty, one hundred, one hundred and fifty grams.

65
00:03:40,767 --> 00:03:43,477
The dependent variable is the one you measure.

66
00:03:43,477 --> 00:03:45,039
It depends on the first one.

67
00:03:45,039 --> 00:03:46,756
Here, the time taken to melt.

68
00:03:46,967 --> 00:03:54,869
And the controlled variables are everything else you deliberately keep the same, so that the result can only be caused by the thing you changed.

69
00:03:55,100 --> 00:04:02,978
So the independent variable causes the dependent variable, and the controlled variables are held still so that nothing else can sneak in.

70
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:09,010
A controlled experiment changes one thing and keeps every other factor the same.

71
00:04:09,010 --> 00:04:12,502
That is the only way to know which change caused the result.

72
00:04:12,733 --> 00:04:16,576
Change two things at once, and the experiment tells you nothing.

73
00:04:16,576 --> 00:04:23,146
If you use a bigger piece of ice and also move it to a warmer room, and it melts faster, which change did it?

74
00:04:23,367 --> 00:04:24,514
You cannot say.

75
00:04:24,514 --> 00:04:30,930
And in an exam, an answer that names the variables but never says which one is controlled loses marks.

76
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:36,151
A question from the exercise at the end of this chapter.

77
00:04:36,151 --> 00:04:39,442
Read the four options carefully, and pause to think.

78
00:04:46,333 --> 00:04:48,118
B, the mass of the ice.

79
00:04:48,118 --> 00:04:54,434
You decide it, so you change it on purpose: fifty, one hundred, one hundred and fifty grams.

80
00:04:54,434 --> 00:04:58,092
The time is what you measure, so that is the dependent variable.

81
00:04:58,092 --> 00:05:03,068
The room temperature and the beaker are held the same, so they are controlled variables.

82
00:05:07,433 --> 00:05:13,606
A laboratory is a room built for experiments, and everything in it carries its own danger.

83
00:05:13,606 --> 00:05:17,407
That is why every bottle and every cupboard carries a sign.

84
00:05:17,633 --> 00:05:20,681
Flammable: it catches fire easily.

85
00:05:20,681 --> 00:05:24,201
Spirit, kerosene, an L P G cylinder.

86
00:05:24,433 --> 00:05:27,729
Corrosive: it attacks skin and cloth.

87
00:05:27,729 --> 00:05:30,339
Strong acids and strong alkalis.

88
00:05:30,567 --> 00:05:34,790
Toxic, or poisonous: mercury, and many pesticides.

89
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:40,402
Harmful or irritant: dilute acids, lime, bleaching powder.

90
00:05:40,633 --> 00:05:45,127
Oxidising: potassium permanganate, hydrogen peroxide.

91
00:05:45,127 --> 00:05:48,535
These give up oxygen readily and make a fire far worse.

92
00:05:48,767 --> 00:05:54,075
And explosive: sodium metal dropped in water, or dry ice sealed in a bottle.

93
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:58,834
Here is the mistake almost every student makes once.

94
00:05:58,834 --> 00:06:02,825
Never put your nose over the mouth of the bottle, and never taste a chemical.

95
00:06:03,033 --> 00:06:10,561
To smell a gas safely, hold the bottle away from you and fan the vapour towards your nose with your open hand.

96
00:06:10,561 --> 00:06:12,793
A little reaches you, and no more.

97
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:14,852
Now the habits.

98
00:06:14,852 --> 00:06:18,765
Read the whole instruction before you begin, and follow it in order.

99
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:23,098
Apron on, goggles on.

100
00:06:23,098 --> 00:06:24,726
Never taste anything.

101
00:06:24,726 --> 00:06:28,593
Never point a test tube at yourself or at a classmate.

102
00:06:28,593 --> 00:06:30,039
Keep the bench clear.

103
00:06:30,039 --> 00:06:35,104
And when you finish, turn the gas off at the burner and at the cylinder, and wash your hands.

104
00:06:36,433 --> 00:06:40,328
Before you touch a single thing, answer three questions.

105
00:06:40,328 --> 00:06:41,786
What am I trying to find out?

106
00:06:41,786 --> 00:06:45,054
What is the danger in this apparatus or this chemical?

107
00:06:45,054 --> 00:06:50,653
And what will I do if something goes wrong, a spill, a burn, or broken glass?

108
00:06:50,653 --> 00:06:56,080
A student who can answer all three works safely, and writes a better report too.

109
00:07:00,433 --> 00:07:06,138
An experiment is a controlled test of an idea, and its report has six parts.

110
00:07:06,138 --> 00:07:10,441
They always come in this order, so a reader can repeat exactly what you did.

111
00:07:10,667 --> 00:07:16,189
Aim, apparatus, procedure, observation, result, conclusion.

112
00:07:16,189 --> 00:07:18,879
Six parts, in that order, every single time.

113
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:22,063
Let us run the whole experiment on paper.

114
00:07:22,063 --> 00:07:26,637
Our aim: to find out how the time taken by ice to melt depends on its mass.

115
00:07:26,867 --> 00:07:35,762
Apparatus: three identical beakers, ice crushed to the same grain size, a balance, a stopwatch, and a thermometer.

116
00:07:35,762 --> 00:07:43,944
Procedure: weigh the ice, note the room temperature, start the watch, stir gently, and stop it when no ice is left.

117
00:07:44,167 --> 00:07:45,757
Now the observations.

118
00:07:45,757 --> 00:07:49,143
Fifty grams: two hundred and forty seconds.

119
00:07:49,143 --> 00:07:51,786
One hundred grams: four hundred and eighty.

120
00:07:51,786 --> 00:07:54,800
One hundred and fifty grams: seven hundred and twenty.

121
00:07:55,033 --> 00:07:56,884
Put those readings on a graph.

122
00:07:56,884 --> 00:07:58,139
Mass across, time up.

123
00:07:58,367 --> 00:07:59,684
And look what happens.

124
00:07:59,684 --> 00:08:04,176
The three points fall on a straight line, and that line goes through zero.

125
00:08:04,176 --> 00:08:06,860
That shape has a name: directly proportional.

126
00:08:07,067 --> 00:08:08,527
Check it with the numbers.

127
00:08:08,527 --> 00:08:11,665
Four eighty over two forty is two.

128
00:08:11,665 --> 00:08:14,816
Seven twenty over two forty is three.

129
00:08:14,816 --> 00:08:19,739
So the time is proportional to the mass, and the hypothesis was supported.

130
00:08:19,967 --> 00:08:22,989
One precaution belongs in every report.

131
00:08:22,989 --> 00:08:33,699
Ice of a different grain size melts at a different speed, so all three samples must be crushed the same way, and stirred equally gently, because stirring warms the mixture.

132
00:08:38,167 --> 00:08:43,676
Research is a careful search for an answer to a question that is not yet settled.

133
00:08:43,676 --> 00:08:47,160
A school project is small, but it follows the same steps.

134
00:08:47,367 --> 00:08:49,712
First, choose a narrow question.

135
00:08:49,712 --> 00:08:53,189
How much water does a household in my ward use in a day?

136
00:08:53,189 --> 00:08:54,375
That can be studied in a week.

137
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:56,971
How can Nepal solve its water problem?

138
00:08:56,971 --> 00:08:58,026
That cannot.

139
00:08:58,026 --> 00:09:02,783
A question that is too big cannot be answered with the evidence you can actually collect.

140
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,546
Then read what is already known, and write down where each fact came from.

141
00:09:07,546 --> 00:09:12,702
Plan how you will collect evidence: measure, count, interview, or survey.

142
00:09:12,933 --> 00:09:14,172
Collect it honestly.

143
00:09:14,172 --> 00:09:17,140
Never change a reading to make the story neater.

144
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:20,201
Then organise it into tables and simple graphs.

145
00:09:20,433 --> 00:09:28,631
And finally, write the report: title, aim, method, findings, conclusion, and a list of sources.

146
00:09:29,333 --> 00:09:30,389
One warning.

147
00:09:30,389 --> 00:09:36,496
Copying three paragraphs from a website and changing a few words is not research.

148
00:09:36,496 --> 00:09:38,679
It is copying, and it teaches you nothing.

149
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:42,261
When you use someone else's finding, say where it came from.

150
00:09:42,261 --> 00:09:48,106
Your own reading, your own count, your own measurement, that is the part that is really yours.

151
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:59,090
A survey collects information from many people or places by asking the same questions of each.

152
00:09:59,090 --> 00:10:02,116
We use it when measuring directly is impossible.

153
00:10:02,333 --> 00:10:07,283
Here is our ward: two thousand households, along eight lanes.

154
00:10:07,283 --> 00:10:09,151
We cannot visit them all in a week.

155
00:10:09,367 --> 00:10:10,749
So we take a sample.

156
00:10:10,749 --> 00:10:15,449
One household in every few, all the way down every lane.

157
00:10:15,449 --> 00:10:19,303
That is a fair sample, because every part of the ward is represented.

158
00:10:19,533 --> 00:10:24,430
A sample made of your own friends, all from one lane, is not fair.

159
00:10:24,430 --> 00:10:27,921
The way you choose the sample matters more than how big it is.

160
00:10:28,733 --> 00:10:29,959
Let us work one out.

161
00:10:29,959 --> 00:10:37,583
A sample of forty households, taken every fifth house on four lanes, and one question asked of an adult in each home.

162
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:44,403
The tally gives twenty two who always separate, twelve who sometimes do, and six who never.

163
00:10:44,403 --> 00:10:45,482
Forty in total.

164
00:10:45,700 --> 00:10:48,775
A bar chart shows the same thing at a glance.

165
00:10:48,775 --> 00:10:52,949
The always bar is the tallest, but it is not a majority on its own.

166
00:10:53,167 --> 00:11:00,607
Twenty two out of forty is zero point five five, so fifty five per cent of the sample always separate their waste.

167
00:11:00,833 --> 00:11:05,001
And now the honest part, which belongs in every survey report.

168
00:11:05,001 --> 00:11:07,827
It shows what people said, not what they do.

169
00:11:07,827 --> 00:11:10,857
And forty households cannot stand for two thousand.

170
00:11:12,967 --> 00:11:16,341
Think about the ward we just drew, with eight lanes.

171
00:11:16,341 --> 00:11:18,580
Which sample would really stand for the whole ward?

172
00:11:25,467 --> 00:11:28,320
B, every fifth house along every lane.

173
00:11:28,320 --> 00:11:32,370
Every lane is represented, so the sample looks like the ward.

174
00:11:32,370 --> 00:11:40,755
Classmates are all from one school, the first forty houses are all from one corner, and the richest lane is one lane only.

175
00:11:40,755 --> 00:11:47,241
A fair sample must cover every part of the population, or the answer describes the sample and not the ward.

176
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:55,690
Every measurement has two parts: a number and a unit.

177
00:11:55,690 --> 00:11:58,112
A length of five means nothing at all.

178
00:11:58,112 --> 00:11:59,692
Five metres means something.

179
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:00,748
Five.

180
00:12:00,748 --> 00:12:01,790
Five what?

181
00:12:01,790 --> 00:12:03,469
Five grains of rice?

182
00:12:03,469 --> 00:12:04,543
Five kilometres?

183
00:12:04,767 --> 00:12:07,138
Add the unit, and it becomes a measurement.

184
00:12:07,367 --> 00:12:09,830
These are the units you will use all year.

185
00:12:09,830 --> 00:12:17,839
Length in metres, mass in kilograms, time in seconds, temperature in kelvin, and electric current in amperes.

186
00:12:18,067 --> 00:12:21,011
Notice the instrument beside each one.

187
00:12:21,011 --> 00:12:24,774
A measurement is only as good as the instrument that made it.

188
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,543
Bigger and smaller units are built with prefixes.

189
00:12:28,543 --> 00:12:31,108
A thousand metres is a kilometre.

190
00:12:31,108 --> 00:12:33,530
A hundred centimetres is a metre.

191
00:12:33,530 --> 00:12:35,656
A thousand grams is a kilogram.

192
00:12:36,467 --> 00:12:40,153
Here is a measurement question of the kind the exam loves.

193
00:12:40,153 --> 00:12:50,175
A stone of mass eighty four grams is lowered into a measuring cylinder holding forty millilitres of water, and the level rises to seventy two.

194
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:51,469
Watch the water.

195
00:12:51,469 --> 00:12:57,213
The stone displaces its own volume, so the rise tells us the volume of the stone.

196
00:12:58,433 --> 00:13:04,281
Volume equals seventy two minus forty, thirty two cubic centimetres.

197
00:13:04,281 --> 00:13:05,846
That is the volume of the stone.

198
00:13:06,067 --> 00:13:14,862
Density is mass over volume: eighty four over thirty two, which is two point six two five grams per cubic centimetre.

199
00:13:15,067 --> 00:13:19,001
Water has a density of one gram per cubic centimetre.

200
00:13:19,001 --> 00:13:22,048
The stone is denser than water, so it sinks.

201
00:13:23,567 --> 00:13:26,511
Two habits protect every reading you take.

202
00:13:26,511 --> 00:13:32,006
Write the unit once, in the head of the column, never beside every number.

203
00:13:32,006 --> 00:13:35,977
And before you write a reading down, ask whether it is sensible.

204
00:13:35,977 --> 00:13:44,571
A classroom is about eight metres long, not eight centimetres, and a school bag weighs a few kilograms, not a few grams.

205
00:13:44,571 --> 00:13:48,182
If the number looks impossible, the unit or the instrument is wrong.

206
00:13:50,367 --> 00:13:52,139
Let us gather it together.

207
00:13:52,139 --> 00:13:57,621
Science is organised knowledge, tested by observation and experiment.

208
00:13:57,621 --> 00:14:07,022
The method is a loop: observe, question, hypothesise, predict, experiment, analyse, conclude, and then repeat.

209
00:14:07,022 --> 00:14:14,496
In a controlled experiment you change the independent variable, measure the dependent one, and hold everything else the same.

210
00:14:14,496 --> 00:14:22,205
In the laboratory: read first, wear your goggles and apron, never taste, never point a tube at anyone.

211
00:14:22,205 --> 00:14:24,991
A report has six parts in a fixed order.

212
00:14:24,991 --> 00:14:29,639
A survey needs a fair sample and an honest statement of its limits.

213
00:14:29,639 --> 00:14:31,408
And every measurement needs a unit.

214
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:39,404
That is scientific learning, the toolkit for the rest of the year.

215
00:14:39,404 --> 00:14:45,276
Next time, we look at the tools themselves: information and communication technology.

216
00:14:45,276 --> 00:14:49,535
Keep asking good questions, and see you in the next lesson on NEBians.

